# AI Pair Programming Workflows

## Pair Programming Tool Landscape

* From Code Generation to a Coding Partner

- @Motivation@: A _strong coding model_ is only half of the problem
  - The other half is _where_ the assistant runs, _what_ it can read, and _what_
    it is allowed to do

- @Question@: Two engineers use the same underlying model and get very different
  results: what differs?

- @Overview@: This lesson covers
  1. The tool landscape and the interaction models behind it
  2. Where the assistant runs: CLI, Desktop, IDE, Web, Mobile
  3. Integrations that extend the assistant past the editor
  4. Working asynchronously, when no human is attached to the session

* Human Pair Programming as the Baseline

// From book Williams et al., "Pair Programming Illuminated" (2002)

- @Definition@: In **pair programming**, two programmers work on one task at one
  workstation
  - _Driver_: Writes the code
  - _Navigator_: Reviews each line, watches for defects, thinks one step ahead
  - Roles rotate during the session

// From paper Williams et al., "Strengthening the Case for Pair Programming"
// (2000)

- @Fact@: A controlled study compared pairs against a solo programmer, not
  against two solo programmers each completing the task independently
  - Pairs spent roughly 15% more total programmer time than the solo baseline
  - Delivered in less elapsed time, since two people worked simultaneously
  - Produced code that passed more of the test cases than solo programmers

- @Key idea@: The benefit comes:
  - From _continuous review_
  - From having to say out loud what the code is supposed to do
  - Not from typing twice

* What Changes with an AI Partner

- @Comparison@: A human navigator versus a model navigator
  - _Availability_: No scheduling cost, available for every task
  - _Speed_: Drafts an entire file in seconds, so human review, not typing,
    becomes the bottleneck
  - _Memory_: No recollection of last week's design discussion unless it was
    written into a file in the repository
  - _Accountability_: Cannot own a decision or be responsible for a defect

- @Key idea@: AI pair programming **inverts the roles**: the model drives, the
  human navigates

- @Remark@: Human-AI Collaboration Patterns cover how much control to hand over
  and when (later lesson)

* Three Interaction Models

- @Definition@: An **interaction model** fixes what the assistant can see and
  what actions it can take on its own

::: columns
:::: {.column width=45%}

- @Overview@
  1. _Autocomplete_: Continues code at the cursor
  2. _Chat_: Answers a scoped request in a side panel
  3. _Agent_: Pursues a goal by calling tools in a loop

::::
:::: {.column width=50%}

// TODO(ai_gp): Make it a bidimensional graph and place the boxes there
```graphviz
digraph InteractionModels {
  bgcolor="transparent";
  pad="0.15";
  splines=spline;
  nodesep=0.30;
  ranksep=0.50;
  rankdir=LR;

  node [shape=box,
        style="rounded,filled",
        fillcolor="#FFFFFF",
        color="#9AA9B8",
        penwidth=1.8,
