  edge [color="#A3B1C0",
        penwidth=1.3,
        arrowhead=vee,
        arrowsize=0.75,
        fontname="Helvetica",
        fontsize=10,
        fontcolor="#7B8794"];

  // autocomplete : cyan   |   chat : teal   |   agent : orange

  Auto  [label=<<b>Autocomplete</b><br/><font point-size="10" color="#1F4E56">Completes a line</font>>,
         fillcolor="#C7ECF0", color="#7CC6D0", fontcolor="#1F4E56"];
  Chat  [label=<<b>Chat</b><br/><font point-size="10" color="#1F4E39">Answers a request</font>>,
         fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
  Agent [label=<<b>Agent</b><br/><font point-size="10" color="#6B4517">Pursues a goal</font>>,
         fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];

  Auto -> Chat -> Agent [label="  more context,\l  more actions\l"];
}
```

::::
:::

- @Key idea@: The three models differ in the _unit of work_ they accept (e.g.,
  from one line to one task)

* Autocomplete Assistance

- @Definition@: **Autocomplete assistance** completes code inline at the cursor,
  using the surrounding file as context
  - Human accepts or rejects changes with a single keystroke

- @Characteristics@
  - Sub-second latency, since the suggestion appears while typing
  - Unit of work is a line or a small block
  - The assistant never executes anything it wrote

// From paper Nguyen et al., "An Empirical Evaluation of GitHub Copilot's
// Code Suggestions" (2022)

- @Fact@: An early empirical evaluation found that the correctness of
  suggestions varied substantially across programming languages for the same
  tool, while the suggested code itself was generally simple to read

- @Cons@: The human supplies all context implicitly, by having the right file
  open at the right cursor position

* Chat Assistance

- @Definition@: **Chat assistance** answers a natural-language request in a side
  panel, scoped to a selection, a file, or a pasted snippet

- @Characteristics@
  - Handles requests autocomplete cannot express
    - E.g., _"explain this function"_, _"rewrite this loop without recursion"_
  - The output is text: The human decides what to apply, and applies it
  - Context is whatever the human selected or pasted

- @Cons@: Transcription overhead
  - The human moves code into the panel and the answer back out
  - Nothing in the loop checks whether the answer actually compiles or passes
    the tests

* Agentic Assistance

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}

- @Definition@: An **agentic assistant** pursues a stated goal by calling
  _tools_, observing the result, and deciding the next step
  - Tool examples are "read a file", 'search the repository", "edit a file", "run
    a command"

- @Characteristics@
  - Unit of work is a task, not a line
  - The assistant gathers its own context instead of receiving it
  - Test output, compiler errors, and grep results re-enter the loop

- @Key idea@: What makes an assistant "agentic" is _feedback from the environment
  inside the loop_, not how good is the model

::::
:::: {.column width=35%}

// TODO(ai_gp): Maybe horizontal
```graphviz
digraph AgentLoop {
  bgcolor="transparent";
  pad="0.15";
  splines=spline;
  nodesep=0.30;
  ranksep=0.50;
  rankdir=TB;

  node [shape=box,
        style="rounded,filled",
        fillcolor="#FFFFFF",
        color="#9AA9B8",
        penwidth=1.8,
        fontname="Helvetica",
        fontcolor="#243B53",
        fontsize=12,
        margin="0.22,0.14",
        height=0.50];

  edge [color="#3A4A5C",
        penwidth=1.6,
        arrowhead=vee,
        arrowsize=0.85,
        fontname="Helvetica",
        fontsize=11,
        fontcolor="#3A4A5C"];

  // goal : orange   |   planning : teal   |   tool call : rose   |   observation : cyan   |   result : blue

  Goal     [label="Goal", fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
  Plan     [label="Plan next step", fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
  CallTool [label="Call a tool\n(read, edit, run, ...)", fillcolor="#F6C6C6", color="#D98C8C", fontcolor="#6B2A2A"];
  Observe  [label="Observe result", fillcolor="#C7ECF0", color="#7CC6D0", fontcolor="#1F4E56"];
  Result   [label="Result", fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];

  Goal -> Plan;
  Plan -> CallTool;
  CallTool -> Observe;
  Observe -> Result [label="  Goal met"];
  Observe -> Plan [label="  Iterate", style=dashed, constraint=false];
}
```

::::
:::

* Autocomplete Vs. Agentic

- The two ends of the interaction-model spectrum are the following:

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Property", "Autocomplete", "Agentic"),
  rows: (
    ("Unit of work", "A line or block", "A whole task"),
    ("Context", "Cursor window, open files", "Gathered via tools"),
    ("Actions", "Propose text only", "Read, edit, run commands"),
    ("Latency", "Milliseconds", "Minutes"),
    ("Verification", "Human eyes, then tests", "Tests inside the loop"),
    ("Cost of a mistake", "Reject one suggestion",
     "Review a multi-file diff"),
  ),
  col-widths: (1fr, 1.2fr, 1.3fr),
)
```

- @Key idea@: Moving right on this table buys larger units of work and pays for
  them with heavier review

* Vibe Coding Vs. Agentic Coding

// From paper Sapkota et al., "Vibe Coding vs. Agentic Coding" (2025)

- @Definition@: **Vibe coding** is conversational, prompt-and-inspect
  development where the human stays inside every iteration and keeps structural
  judgment

- @Definition@: **Agentic coding** delegates a goal to a system that plans,
  executes, and validates on its own, reporting back at the end

- @Comparison@
  - _Vibe coding_: Exploration, prototyping, unfamiliar territory
  - _Agentic coding_: Repeatable tasks with a mechanical success criterion

// From paper Wang et al., "AI Agentic Programming: A Survey" (2025)

- @Remark@: Agent internals (e.g., planning, tool use, memory, and multi-agent
  orchestration) are covered in detail in following chapters

* Claude Code

// From book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/claude_code.md/Where to
// Run Claude Code

- @Definition@: **Claude Code** is a terminal-first agentic assistant
  - Available on desktop, in IDE (e.g., VS Code and JetBrains), on the Web, and
    on mobile

- @Characteristics@
  - Default interaction is agentic (e.g., read, edit, and run commands)
  - Permission prompts gate actions the user has not pre-authorized
  - Repository-level instruction files supply standing conventions
  - Extensible through MCP servers, and scriptable through the Agent SDK

- @Example@: _"run the test suite, fix the failures, and open a pull request"_
  is one single request

* GitHub Copilot

// From book Laster, "Learning GitHub Copilot" (2025)
// From internet resource GitHub, "GitHub Copilot Documentation"

- @Definition@: **GitHub Copilot** began as inline autocomplete inside the
  editor and now spans:
  - Autocomplete
  - Chat
  - Agent modes across IDEs
  - A command-line interface
  - The GitHub web interface

- @Characteristics@
  - The same product exposes all three interaction models, selected per task
  - Tightly coupled to the code host, so issues and pull requests are natural
    entry points

- @Key idea@: With a multi-mode tool, the mode chosen for a task matters more
  than the brand on the tool

* Cursor

// From internet resource Cursor, "Cursor Docs"

- @Definition@: **Cursor** is an AI-first code editor, where completion and an
  agent mode live in the same window as the diff being reviewed

- @Characteristics@
  - Editor-centric: The assistant and the review surface are the same program
  - _Rules_ files carry project conventions into every request
  - MCP support and a command-line interface extend it past the editor

- @Intuition@: Cursor optimizes the loop _propose $\to$ see the diff $\to$
  accept_, which is why its natural home is the editor rather than the shell

* The Three Tools Side by Side

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Tool", "Primary surface", "Default interaction", "Extension"),
  rows: (
    ("Claude Code", "Terminal", "Agentic", "MCP, Agent SDK"),
    ("GitHub Copilot", "IDE and code host", "Autocomplete to agent",
     "MCP, extensions"),
    ("Cursor", "Custom editor", "Autocomplete plus agent", "MCP, rules"),
  ),
  col-widths: (1fr, 1.2fr, 1.3fr, 1.1fr),
)
```

- @Remark@: All three
  - Can use the same class of models
  - Can converge on similar features
  - Differ on the "interaction surface" and the workflow that surface encourages

* Evidence From Deployment

// TODO(ai_gp): Add more information

// From paper Agarwal et al., "AI IDEs or Autonomous Agents? Measuring the
// Impact of Coding Agents on Software Development" (2026)

- @Fact@: An empirical comparison of IDE-embedded assistants against autonomous
  coding agents finds that the two affect productivity and code quality
  differently
  - Numbers measured for one interaction model do not transfer to the other
// From paper Peralta et al., "Why Are Agentic Pull Requests Merged or
// Rejected? An Empirical Study" (2026)

- @Fact@: A study of agent-authored pull requests across several tools examines
  why such pull requests are merged or rejected in real review and CI workflows

## Where to Run the Assistant

* Why the Surface Matters

- @Definition@: A **surface** is the program through which the AI assistant is
  driven
  - E.g., a terminal, a desktop app, an IDE, a browser, or a phone

- @Problem@: The surface, not the model, decides three practical things
  1. What context is available by default, e.g., the open file, the shell
     history, the repository
  2. How a proposed change is reviewed, e.g., a rendered diff or a wall of text
  3. How long a session can live, e.g., until the laptop sleeps, or beyond it

// From book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/claude_code.md/Where to
// Run Claude Code

- @Overview@: CLI, Desktop, VS Code, JetBrains, Web, Mobile

* CLI: Terminal and Scripting

// From book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/claude_code.md/Where to
// Run Claude Code

- @Characteristics@
  - Full feature set, since the terminal is the assistant's native home
  - Composes with everything else in the shell
    - E.g., pipes, exit codes, scripts
  - Supports the Agent SDK and third-party model providers

- @Example@: The same assistant invoked from a shell script, a Makefile target,
  or a CI job, with no human attached to the session

- @Pros@: The only surface that is directly programmable, so it is the one that
  scales to repeated and automated work

- @Cons@: Review happens as text in a scrollback buffer, not as a rendered,
  navigable diff

* Desktop and IDE Surfaces

// From book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/claude_code.md/Where to
// Run Claude Code

- @Variants@
  - _Desktop_: Diff viewer and app preview, several sessions side by side
  - _VS Code_: Inline diffs in the editor and an integrated terminal
  - _JetBrains_: Diff viewer and sharing of the current selection with the
    assistant

- @Characteristics@: The change is rendered where the code already lives, so
  accepting or rejecting it is a local, low-cost decision

- @Key idea@: These surfaces optimize for _reviewing_, keeping the human next
  to the diff rather than next to the transcript

* Web and Mobile: Long-Running and Remote

// From book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/claude_code.md/Where to
// Run Claude Code

- @Variants@
  - _Web_: Sessions run in a managed environment and keep running after the user
    disconnects
  - _Mobile_: Start and monitor tasks away from the computer, through cloud
    sessions or by controlling a session running locally

- @Characteristics@: The session outlives the laptop, so a task is no longer
  bounded by one sitting at one desk

- @Key idea@: Once a session can outlive the human's attention, pair programming
  turns into _delegation plus inspection_

* Surfaces Compared

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Surface", "Best for", "Trade-off"),
  rows: (
    ("CLI", "Scripting, automation, repeated tasks",
     "Text-only review"),
    ("Desktop", "Visual diff review, parallel sessions",
     "Tied to one machine"),
    ("VS Code / JetBrains", "Editing with inline diffs",
     "Context limited to the project open in the IDE"),
    ("Web", "Long-running and offline tasks",
     "Runs outside the local environment"),
    ("Mobile", "Starting and monitoring work while away",
     "Small screen makes real review impractical"),
  ),
  col-widths: (1.1fr, 1.6fr, 1.5fr),
)
```

* Choosing a Surface

::: columns
:::: {.column width=45%}

- @Steps@: Pick the surface based on the task
  1. _"Repeatable or scripted?"_ $\to$ CLI
  2. _"Needs close diff review?"_ $\to$ IDE or Desktop
  3. _"Longer than one sitting?"_ $\to$ Web
  4. _"Away from the machine?"_ $\to$ Mobile

::::
:::: {.column width=50%}

// TODO(ai_gp): Make it look better and make it a template from it

```graphviz
digraph SurfaceChoice {
  rankdir=TB;
  nodesep=0.3;
