  Web    [label="Web", fillcolor="#B2E2B2"];
  Mob    [label="Mobile", fillcolor="#B2E2B2"];
  Task -> G1;
  G1 -> Cli [label=" yes"];
  G1 -> G2  [label=" no"];
  G2 -> Ide [label=" yes"];
  G2 -> G3  [label=" no"];
  G3 -> Web [label=" yes"];
  G3 -> Mob [label=" no"];
}
```

::::
:::

- A single task can also move across surfaces
  - E.g., started from a phone, executed on a desktop, reviewed in an IDE

* Evidence: Command-Line Agents at Scale

// TODO(ai_gp): Describe better the results of the paper

// From paper Murphy-Hill et al., "Adoption and Impact of Command-Line AI
// Coding Agents" (2026)

- @Fact@: A large-scale study of an early enterprise rollout of command-line
  coding agents reports what drove adoption across thousands of developers, and
  measures the effect on pull-request throughput

- @Key idea@: The terminal surface is not a niche preference for a few
  engineers, it is a deployment target that organizations roll out and measure

- @Remark@: Adoption is uneven across teams and tasks, so surface-level averages
  hide large differences between the workflows underneath

## Extending the Workflow

* Beyond the Editor

- @Motivation@: Most of the work around a change happens outside the editor
  - E.g., pull requests, CI runs, chat threads, browsers, tickets

- @Problem@: An assistant that only sees the editor forces the human to act as a
  courier
  - E.g., copying a CI log into a prompt and a fix back out

- @Definition@: An **integration** connects the assistant to an external system,
  so that system's state becomes readable context and its actions become
  available tools

// From book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/claude_code.md/Connect
// Your Tools

- @Overview@: CI pipelines, automated pull-request review, chat, the browser,
  and MCP servers for everything else

* CI Integration

// From book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/claude_code.md/Connect
// Your Tools

- @Definition@: A **CI integration** runs the assistant as a pipeline step in the
  continuous integration workflow (e.g., GitHub Actions or GitLab CI), triggered
  by a push, a pull request, a comment, or a label

- @Characteristics@
  - Non-interactive: Nobody is present to answer a permission prompt
  - Credentials must be scoped in advance to what the job may touch
  - The only useful output is an artifact: A comment, a commit, or a pull
    request

- @Remark@: Pipeline design, approval gating, and release automation are covered
  in detail in next lessons

* Automated Pull-Request Review

// From book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/claude_code.md/Connect
// Your Tools

- @Definition@: An **automated review** integration reads the diff of a pull
  request and posts inline comments before a human reviewer opens it

- @Pros@: A first pass catches the mechanical findings, so human attention goes
  to design and trade-offs

- @Cons@: False positives are expensive
  - A reviewer who starts to ignore the bot, loses the value of the true findings
    too

- @Remark@: What automated review actually catches, and how to combine it with
  human review, is covered in later chapters

* Chat and Browser Integrations

// From book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/claude_code.md/Connect
// Your Tools

- @Variants@
  - _Chat_: The assistant responds to a mention in a channel, so a task can start
    in the thread where the problem was reported and the result lands back in the
    same thread
  - _Browser_: The assistant drives a real browser to exercise a user flow
    - E.g., fill a form, check what a page actually renders

- @Intuition@: A browser integration closes the gap between _"the tests pass"_
  and _"the feature works in the product"_

- @Key idea@: Integrations move the assistant to where the evidence and the
  conversation already are, instead of moving both to the assistant

* MCP: One Protocol for Many Tools

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}

- @Problem@: $N$ assistants and $M$ tools need $N \times M$ bespoke integrations
// From internet resource Model Context Protocol, "Specification"
// From internet resource Anthropic, "Introducing the Model Context Protocol"

- @Definition@: The **Model Context Protocol (MCP)** is an open protocol in
  which a client (the assistant) connects to servers that expose tools,
  resources, and prompts through one uniform interface

- @Key idea@: $N + M$ implementations instead of $N \times M$

::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

```graphviz
digraph MCP {
  rankdir=LR;
  nodesep=0.25;
  node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica",
        fontsize=9, penwidth=1.4];
  Client [label="Assistant\n(MCP client)", fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
  S1 [label="Issue tracker", fillcolor="#A0D6D1"];
  S2 [label="Database", fillcolor="#A0D6D1"];
  S3 [label="Docs search", fillcolor="#A0D6D1"];
  S4 [label="Internal API", fillcolor="#A0D6D1"];
  Client -> S1 [dir=both];
  Client -> S2 [dir=both];
  Client -> S3 [dir=both];
  Client -> S4 [dir=both];
}
```

::::
:::

* MCP in Practice

// From paper Chen et al., "Understanding How Enterprises Adopt the Model
// Context Protocol for LLM-Driven Software Engineering" (2026)

- @Fact@: Practitioner interviews on enterprise MCP adoption report that the
  hard part is organizational, not at the level of implementation
  - E.g., Finding trustworthy servers, wiring authentication, and governing what
    each server may do

// From paper Hou et al., "Model Context Protocol (MCP): Landscape, Security
// Threats, and Future Research Directions" (2025)

- @Challenges@: Every connected server widens both the action space and the
  attack surface
  - Over-broad credentials handed to a server that only needed read access
  - Content returned by a server is untrusted input that reaches the model

- @Key idea@: Connecting an integration means _granting a permission_, so
  the two decisions should be made together

